---
// Hero candidate 01 — "The diary fills".
import Shell from '../layouts/Shell.astro';
import DiaryHero from '../components/hero/DiaryHero.astro';
import { studio } from '../data/studio.js';
import '../styles/hero-diary.css';

const mailto = `mailto:${studio.contact.email}?subject=Website%20enquiry`;
const ownSiteHost = studio.proof.ownSite.url.replace(/^https?:\/\//, '');
---
<Shell
  title="I build websites that fill your diary. Stacked Out"
  variant="diary"
  blurb="Websites for electricians and solar installers, built by a spark with 25 years on the tools."
>
  <DiaryHero />

  <!-- The story. Numbers are the brand's punctuation, so they get set at size
       and carry the section rather than a paragraph doing it. Both are the
       founder's own figures, the only two numbers on this page. -->
  <section class="band band--story">
    <div class="shell">
      <h2 class="band__h">
        So I learnt to build them myself.
      </h2>

      <div class="story">
        <dl class="story__figures">
          <div class="story__figure">
            <dt class="label">Years on the tools</dt>
            <dd class="figure story__n">{studio.founder.yearsOnTools}</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="story__figure">
            <dt class="label">Working for myself</dt>
            <dd class="figure story__n">{studio.founder.yearsSelfEmployed}</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class="story__prose">
          <p>
            Every firm that rang me about marketing wanted to talk about
            impressions and reach. Not one of them could tell you what an EICR
            is, or why the next town over can be a worse job than one twenty
            miles up the road.
          </p>
          <!-- Was "So I built my own instead", which opened with the same
               word as the heading and restated the claim the heading had
               already made. The heading says he learnt to build them; this
               paragraph should move on to the proof, not say it again. -->
          <p>
            Mine is the only one I can show you yet. Go and pull it apart.
            I’d rather you judged me on that than on a pitch deck.
          </p>
          <p class="story__link">
            <a href={studio.proof.ownSite.url} rel="noopener">{ownSiteHost}</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- What is not known yet, said out loud. The guide wants a price on the page
       and there isn't one agreed, so the gap is shown as a gap. Placeholders,
       never guesses. -->
  <section class="band band--close">
    <div class="shell">
      <h2 class="band__h">What it costs</h2>
      <p class="band__lead">
        A number belongs right here. There isn’t one agreed yet, so you get a
        gap instead of a guess. Ask and you’ll get a straight answer.
      </p>

      <ul class="gaps">
        <li class="gap">
          <span class="label gap__k">Price</span>
          <span class="gap__v">Not set yet</span>
        </li>
        <li class="gap">
          <span class="label gap__k">Turnaround</span>
          <span class="gap__v">Not set yet</span>
        </li>
        <li class="gap">
          <span class="label gap__k">Other trades’ sites</span>
          <span class="gap__v">None to show yet</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="gaps__note">
        Three placeholders, held open until there’s a real answer. When there
        are other trades’ sites to show, they’ll be here with the trade named.
      </p>

      <div class="close">
        <a class="btn btn--action" href={mailto}>Book yours in</a>
        <p class="close__addr">{studio.contact.email}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Shell>
